3.3. An Analogue of Fermat’s Little Theorem

We can derive a poly-Bernoulli cohort to Fermat’s Little Theorem, which states that for any
positive integer a and prime number p,
                                      ap ≡ a (mod p).

   We will start with the main lemma of [1].
Lemma 5 (ABR 2005). Let S be a finite set, let p be a prime number, let f (x) : S → S be
a function that has the property f p (x) = x for any x in S, and let F be the number of fixed
points. Then
                                      |S| ≡ |F | (mod p).

   One could view the function f as a directed graph on elements in S consisting of disjoint
length p cycles and loops.
Theorem 4 (Row Ordered Fermat’s Little Theorem). Given a prime number p, and
a positive integer n,
                          Bn(−p) ≡ 2n (mod p).                            (3)

Proof. Let S be the set of n × p Lonesum (0,1)-matrices. Let f be the function that rotates
the columns of a matrix once to the left. The matrices that are fixed points in this function
are those consisting of all one and all zero rows. There are 2n such matrices, so let F = 2n .
                                                                                    (−p)
From the above lemma |S| ≡ |F | (mod p). Substituting for S and F we get Bn              ≡ 2n
(mod p).
